The bad squares are in $[L_1, R_1] \cup [L_2, R_2] \cup \dots \cup [L_M, R_M]$.
The good squares are $[1, L_1-1] \cup [R_1+1, L_2-1] \cup \dots \cup [R_{M-1}+1, L_M-1] \cup [R_M+1, N]$.
Note that if $R_i + 1 > L_{i+1} - 1$, i.e., $R_i + 1 \ge L_{i+1}$, the intervals might touch or overlap, but the problem says $R_i < L_{i+1}$, so there is at least one good square between any two bad intervals.
