If $u, v \in S$, then $d(u), d(v)$ are even. If there is an edge $(u, v)$, then $|d(u) - d(v)| \le 1$, so $d(u) = d(v)$.
If $u, v \in T$, then $d(u), d(v)$ are odd. If there is an edge $(u, v)$, then $d(u) = d(v)$.

Wait, the condition is simply about the parity of the shortest path distance.
Let's consider the bipartite property.
If a graph is bipartite, we can partition vertices into two sets $A$ and $B$ such that all edges are between $A$ and $B$.
If the graph is bipartite, then for any vertex $v$, the distance from 1 has the same parity as the set $v$ belongs to (assuming 1 is in $A$).
Specifically, if the graph is bipartite with parts $A$ and $B$, and $1 \in A$, then for all $v \in A$, $d(v)$ is even, and for all $v \in B$, $d(v)$ is odd.
In this case, the condition $|\{v \mid d(v) \text{ is even}\}| = |\{v \mid d(v) \text{ is odd}\}|$ becomes $|A| = |B| = N/2$.
So, any bipartite graph with partition sizes $N/2, N/2$ satisfies the condition.
However, the condition is not restricted to bipartite graphs.
